      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cottleville was laid out in 1839 when Lorenzo Cottle parceled his land along the Dardenne River and named the town after his father and grandfather.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottleville%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "During the Civil War, Cottleville hosted Camp Krekel, a recruiting camp that drew soldiers from the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofcottleville.com/cottleville-history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The historic Boone’s Lick Road, now Missouri State Route N, began as a trail from St. Charles and passed through Cottleville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofcottleville.com/cottleville-history/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A post office has operated in Cottleville since 1843, marking its continuous community presence.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
